bevy-horizon: NAK_DEBUG=sink and NAK_DEBUG=remat, fewer live registers in fragment shaders

For mesa-switch (https://github.com/danfromtico/mesa-switch, by danfromtico and NaGaa95; MIT)
at d4a00ea0ab3f59afb967cc5d779e4263d237bd77, after 0001 to 0004. scripts/build_nvk.sh applies
it; this text before the first "diff --git" line is ignored by git apply.

NAK_DEBUG=sink runs nir_opt_sink and nir_opt_move on a fragment shader's inputs just before
nak_nir_lower_fs_inputs, so each input is interpolated where it is first used rather than at
the top of the shader. An IPA gives the same value wherever it runs, and NAK lowers discard to
demote, so moving it changes no output.

NAK_DEBUG=remat adds nak_nir_rematerialize_bools, run after the last nir_opt_cse. A switch
becomes one "selector == k" compare per case. CSE leaves them all in the switch's block, where
the default test uses them, so each stays live until its case is reached; Maxwell has seven
predicates and NAK spills the rest to general registers. The pass clones each compare against a
constant, and any iand/ior/ixor/inot of them up to three deep, into every other block that uses
it, so only the selector stays live. nir_opt_rematerialize_compares does this only for compares
used by nothing but bcsel and if, which a switch's compares are not.

On Skate 3's world fragment shader (SM53), NAK_DEBUG=stats went from 3708 instructions, 64 GPRs
and 60 spills to 3468 instructions and no spills with remat. With sink as well (and the game
reading one late vec4 where it is used) it needs 56 GPRs, so 36 warps fit rather than 32. The
water shader went from 128 GPRs and 168 spills to 112 and none. No shader in the game's set got
longer or used more registers. Both passes only move or repeat instructions without side
effects, so every result is unchanged.

Both are opt-in, like xbar. NVK's shader cache key includes the NAK_DEBUG flags, so shaders
built with and without them are cached separately.

